# Agent Session Startup

When you are acting as `@architect`, `@builder`, `@tester`, or `@router`, your **very first action** must be to read your own profile file, then follow its own "Session Start" section in full — this is the single canonical source for your startup read sequence, so this file does not duplicate it. After that, check for `workspace.config.json` per below.

## File paths — hub workspace root only

This workspace has multiple root folders. Agent profiles and memory files both live in the **hub root** (the folder containing `.cursor/agents/`) — NOT in project repo roots.

| Agent | Profile path | Personalisation |
|-------|-------------|----------------|
| `@architect` | `<hub>/.cursor/agents/architect.md` | `<hub>/workspace.config.json` if present |
| `@builder`   | `<hub>/.cursor/agents/builder.md`   | `<hub>/workspace.config.json` if present |
| `@tester`    | `<hub>/.cursor/agents/tester.md`    | `<hub>/workspace.config.json` if present |
| `@router`    | `<hub>/.cursor/agents/router.md`    | `<hub>/workspace.config.json` if present |

Replace `<hub>` with the actual hub folder name (e.g. `agistra.dev`).

Read the profile file first. It contains a "Session Start" section listing every file you must read next (memory, always-on skills, any agent-specific bootstrap step) in the exact order to read them — execute that sequence in full before writing a single word of response or issuing any other tool call. After the profile's own "Session Start" sequence is complete, check whether `<hub>/workspace.config.json` exists; if present, read it for personalisation context. If absent, proceed without it — this is normal, not an error.

## Why the profile file is required

| File | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `.cursor/agents/<agent>.md` | Behavioral contract — identity, skills, decision rules, output formats, absolute prohibitions, AND the authoritative "Session Start" read sequence (memory file, always-on skills) |
| `workspace.config.json` | Personalisation context — user name/role, organisation, per-agent display names |

Do not hand-list memory or skill files here — the profile's own "Session Start" section is the single source of truth for that sequence, and duplicating it in this file risks the two drifting out of sync.

## Common failure pattern to avoid

❌ **Wrong** — looks for the profile in the project repo workspace root:
> *[Read tool call: `<project>/.cursor/agents/builder.md`]* → file not found

❌ **Wrong** — reads the profile but stops instead of following its "Session Start" section:
> *[Read tool call: `<hub>/.cursor/agents/builder.md`]*
> *[starts implementing immediately, skipping memory and always-on skills]*

❌ **Wrong** — completes the profile's "Session Start" sequence but skips `workspace.config.json` when it exists:
> *[Read tool call: `<hub>/.cursor/agents/builder.md`]*
> *[follows Session Start: memory + always-on skills]*
> *[proceeds without checking `workspace.config.json`]*

✅ **Correct** — profile read first, its full "Session Start" sequence executed, then the config check:
> *[Read tool call: `<hub>/.cursor/agents/builder.md`]*
> *[follows Session Start section exactly: memory/builder.md, then every always-on skill it names, in order]*
> *[Read tool call: `<hub>/workspace.config.json`]* (if present)
> *[task work begins]*

## Correct invocation prompts

```text
@architect Read <hub>/.cursor/agents/architect.md and follow its "Session Start" section in full, then check <hub>/workspace.config.json if present. Then: [instruction]
@builder   Read <hub>/.cursor/agents/builder.md   and follow its "Session Start" section in full, then check <hub>/workspace.config.json if present. Then: implement projects/<project>/task_N_todo_<slug>.md
@tester    Read <hub>/.cursor/agents/tester.md    and follow its "Session Start" section in full, then check <hub>/workspace.config.json if present. Then: QA projects/<project>/task_N_todo_<slug>.md
@router    Read <hub>/.cursor/agents/router.md    and follow its "Session Start" section in full, then check <hub>/workspace.config.json if present. Then: classify and route: [inbound message]
```

## Bootstrap Self-Check

After the profile's own "Session Start" sequence completes, that same sequence tells the agent to check the Bootstrap Self-Check trigger defined in `skills/agent-foundations/SKILL.md`. That shared skill — not this file — owns the full protocol (trigger condition, the 7-point report, Architect-only fan-out, persistence). This file does not re-implement it.

## Version Check

On the first message of each session, run the update checker silently from the hub root:

```
node pipelines/deploy/lib/version-check.js
```

- `{"status": "update-available", "local": "...", "remote": "..."}` → inform the user once, briefly: "Hub release {remote} is available; you're on {local}. Run `npm run version:check` for details."
- `{"status": "up-to-date"}` → say nothing
- `{"status": "offline"}` or `{"status": "no-remote-version"}` → say nothing (silent non-failure)
- Any error or missing file → say nothing

The user can also say "check for updates" at any time to force a check.

## Session end — memory discipline

Before closing a session, check whether any decisions, corrections, or new context were established. If yes, update the HOT section in `memory/<agent>.md` before closing.

Do not wait for the team lead to ask. Write first, respond second — this is the WAL protocol defined in `skills/agent-foundations/SKILL.md`.

If nothing significant happened (lookup session, read-only work), explicitly acknowledge: "No significant context to save this session."
